5-6 (chow)\n- T6-7-8 (if I had T8)\n- T7-8-9 (if I had T8)\n- So not possible. \n\nAnother angle: Maybe I can form a hand with two chows and two pairs. For example:\n\n- T4-5-6 (chow)\n- T6-7-7 (pair and a single)\n- T7-7 (another pair)\n- T9 (single)\n- Not enough. \n\nWait, with 14 tiles, four melds and a pair. Let's count:\n\n- T4-5-6 (chow) = 3 tiles\n- T6-7-8 (chow) = 3 tiles\n- T7-8-9 (chow) = 3 tiles\n- T9 (single) + another pair. \n\nBut I don't have T8. \n\nThis is not working. Maybe I need to discard a tile that's not part of a chow. B9 is a terminal, so maybe discard it. \n\nSo the best action is to Play B9. That way, I can focus on the T tiles for possible chows. Even though it's a terminal, it's not part of any chow, so discarding it might be safe.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding B9 allows focusing on T-suit chows and potential pairs, maximizing concealed hand points.]\nReason: [B9 is a terminal tile not part of any chow. Discarding it preserves T4-5-6 chow and T6/T7 pairs, improving chances for All Chows or Pure Shifted Chows. Concealed hand (4 points) combined with potential chows gives better scoring flexibility.]\nAnswer: Play B9"}
